 - `siblingsAcceptingOption` (fb#308) — sibling commands in the same domain whose spec accepts the flag. Derived from `COMMAND_SPECS`, tier-gated, capped at 3; a leaf named after the flag wins over catalogue order. Suppressed by a same-command `didYouMean` (fb#443/449) — with one exception (fb#1731): when the guess came from `FLAG_SYNONYMS` (not spelling) AND a sibling accepts the typed flag verbatim while ALSO owning the guessed one, that sibling is reported alongside the guess, because the domain provably treats the pair as two concepts (`ib keikka` `--asiakas` = tenant vs `--customer` = the order's customer — the pre-fb#1641 dead end). A plain verbatim sibling is NOT enough: in all 22 catalogue pairs where a synonym guess coexists with one, the sibling's flag means something else (`person day set --text` is a note body), so it would be noise.
 - `siblingsAcceptingSynonym` (fb#388) — the same scan retried under synonym spellings, for when the capability lives on a sibling under a *different* name. Sets `acceptedAs` on the envelope, because those commands do NOT accept the rejected spelling and `acceptedBy` alone would misreport that. Suppressed when a same-command did-you-mean already fired.
 - `GROUP_SIBLING_DOMAINS` (fb#343) — curated `company`↔`customer` pair (two views of one `asiakas`). Curated, not derived: nearly every domain owns a `get`, so a scan would suggest noise.
+- `nestedGroupTwins` (fb#1640) — the same-named GROUP under a sibling subgroup owns the verb (`ib jerry request resend` → `ib jerry admin request resend`). Derived; needs a nested group (depth ≥ 3), so it never overlaps the curated pairs.
+- `ROOT_FEATURE_DOMAINS` (fb#1708) — a ROOT token that names a FEATURE, not a domain: `ib grid` → `ib palkki`, with a `why` naming the palkki/schedule/keikka split. Curated, root-only (under a group the token is a verb guess), tier-gated on whole-domain visibility. The three curated layers are disjoint by depth, so they are concatenated without ordering.
 - `descendantsOwningVerb` (fb#379, fb#383) — the verb lives in a child subgroup (`ib keikka assign` → `ib keikka drivers assign`). At the ROOT it returns the **complete** owner list or nothing (`ROOT_MAX_OWNERS = 4`), never a truncation — a generic verb like `list` owns ~28 leaves and three arbitrary ones would be noise.
 - `descendantsOwningCompoundVerb` (fb#1020, fb#1154) — the token is COMPOUND (`company-search`). Three rules, in order: **A.** spelled with spaces it IS a visible path (`ib jerry admin-request-stats` → `ib jerry admin request stats`) → that path, whatever its depth; **B.** the prefix or the verb names a visible child of this group (`ib keikka drivers-list` → the real `drivers` subgroup; `ib customer company-list` → the direct `list`) → SILENT, and `compoundChildOf` makes that child the `didYouMean` (edit distance alone landed on `dead-list`); **C.** otherwise the deep scan, answering only on a SINGLE in-group owner (`ib jerry company-search` → `ib jerry admin search`) — a compound token is a weaker signal than a real verb, and the ambiguous pairs are all generic CRUD (`ib jerry ~ list` ×5). Before fb#1154 the layer had only rule C and so inherited the depth≥2 rule from the exact layer, which meant a DIRECT child could never be the answer and the actionable sentence aimed at a deep owner over the obvious sibling in 7 catalogue cases.
